.TH ERRSTR 2
.SH NAME
errstr \- the name of an error number
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "errstr :: proc(answer: i64) -> string"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every system call answers its result as a signed number: zero or more is
success, and a negative number is
.B \-errno ,
one of the codes
.B sys/vectra9
defines as its
.B Errno
type.
.I Errstr
takes a call's own
.I answer
and names the error: a non-negative
.I answer
names the empty string, and a negative one names its errno in full
capitals, such as
.BR EBADF .
An errno
.B sys/vectra9
does not name prints as
.BR errno .
.PP
Unlike Plan 9's
.IR errstr ,
there is no per-process buffer to swap a string into and no
.I rerrstr
or
.I werrstr
to set one: the number a call answered is the whole of what failed, and
.I errstr
is a pure translation of it, called with the number in hand rather than
read out of hidden state.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/main.odin
.br
.B sys/vectra9/errors.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Errstr
always succeeds.
